{
  "additional_concerns": [
    "The legacy-processing provision preserves an application process. It does not expressly establish permanent substantive disqualification from relief. Candidates that turn pending status at commencement into a false relief result require additional authority; later decisions, withdrawal and resubmission remain unresolved.",
    "The nonlegacy candidate avoids deciding legacy relief by excluding those applications from scope. Its OUT_OF_SCOPE result must not be interpreted as denial of relief.",
    "The specific commencement date supports the date comparison, but the introductory immediate-effect language and the legally relevant assessment event should be reconciled before treating assessment date as a universal implementation test.",
    "The substantive-only candidate deliberately leaves application processing undecided. Its true result cannot establish actual procedural availability of relief.",
    "Home compliance and the novel-feature, complex-feature and possible-policy-implication classifications remain supplied judgments. The formula does not establish those facts or their regulatory thresholds.",
    "No additional home-supervision condition is expressly imposed by item (ii). The introductory purpose statement and item (i)'s supervision condition do not alone justify adding one.",
    "The supplied missing dependency IDs have no identifying descriptions or source hashes. They cannot reliably be assigned to the FAQs or existing approval process."
  ],
